### Exercise the API by hand.
#
# ── Setup ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   1. Start the database (from the repo root):
#        docker compose up -d --wait
#   2. Start the app over HTTPS (the auth cookie is Secure, so plain HTTP won't sign in):
#        dotnet run --project src/ShareExpenses --launch-profile https
#   3. Run the requests below top to bottom. The VS Code REST Client and the
#      JetBrains HTTP Client both keep the sign-in cookie between requests.
#
#   Dev certificate expires 2026-11-13. If HTTPS fails, renew it:
#        dotnet dev-certs https --clean && dotnet dev-certs https --trust
#
# ── Inspecting the data ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   Open a psql shell (from the repo root):
#        docker compose exec postgres psql -U share_expenses -d share_expenses
#
#   Inside psql (\x auto gives one-field-per-line output for wide rows, \q quits):
#        \dn                      -- schemas: ledger (Marten), identity (EF)
#        \dt ledger.*             -- event store tables
#
#        -- the event stream, in order
#        select seq_id, stream_id, version, type, data, timestamp
#        from ledger.mt_events order by seq_id;
#
#        -- one row per stream (per group)
#        select id, version, created from ledger.mt_streams;
#
#        -- events for one group, pretty-printed
#        select version, type, jsonb_pretty(data)
#        from ledger.mt_events where stream_id = '<groupId>' order by version;
#
#        -- users
#        select "Id", "Email" from identity."AspNetUsers";
#
#   Or run a single query without opening a shell:
#        docker compose exec postgres psql -U share_expenses -d share_expenses \
#          -c "select seq_id, type, data from ledger.mt_events order by seq_id;"

@host = https://localhost:7194
@email = alice@example.com

### 1. Sign in (Development only — stand-in for the magic link). Sets the cookie.
# @name signIn
POST {{host}}/dev/sign-in
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}"
}

### 2. Create a group. Expect 201 with groupId and memberId.
# Then check ledger.mt_events: group_created, member_added, member_claimed.
# @name createGroup
POST {{host}}/api/groups
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Lisbon trip",
  "currency": "GBP",
  "displayName": "Alice"
}

### 3. Rejected: unknown currency. Expect 400 with the reason, and no new events.
POST {{host}}/api/groups
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Lisbon trip",
  "currency": "XYZ",
  "displayName": "Alice"
}

### 4. Add a placeholder member to the group from step 2. Expect 201 with memberId.
# Uses step 2's response. That reference syntax is the VS Code REST Client's; in
# the JetBrains client, replace {{groupId}} with the id from step 2 by hand.
@groupId = {{createGroup.response.body.$.groupId}}
POST {{host}}/api/groups/{{groupId}}/members
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "displayName": "Bob"
}

### 5. Rejected: duplicate name (case-insensitive). Expect 400.
POST {{host}}/api/groups/{{groupId}}/members
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "displayName": " bob "
}

### 6. Unknown group. Expect 404 — the same answer a non-member gets.
POST {{host}}/api/groups/01a0f734-90c1-761a-87e3-07c09ef9be9f/members
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "displayName": "Bob"
}

### 7. Health
GET {{host}}/health
